meta {
  name: History - Get profile section history
  type: http
  seq: 6
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/v1/profile-sections/history?employeeInternalID={{employeeInternalID}}&profileSection=PERSONAL
  body: none
  auth: none
}

params:query {
  employeeInternalID: {{employeeInternalID}}
  profileSection: PERSONAL
  recordIdentity:
}

headers {
  X-Api-Key: {{apiKey}}
  X-Company-ID: {{companyId}}
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

tests {
  test("status is ok/rejected/error", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(["ok", "rejected", "error"]).to.include(body.status);
  });

  test("history entries never carry a section value or salt", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    if (body.status === "ok" && body.history && body.history.length > 0) {
      const entry = body.history[0];
      expect(entry).to.not.have.property("newValue");
      expect(entry).to.not.have.property("salt");
    }
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /v1/profile-sections/history

  The one read route the bridge exposes: returns the on-chain transaction
  history for an employee, optionally scoped to one profile section.

  Source: `integration-bridge/internal/pipeline/history.go`.

  ## Query parameters
  | Param | Required | Notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `employeeInternalID` | yes | Resolved server-side to the on-chain pseudonym via `keystore.EmployeeKeyStore.GetOrCreateEmployeeKey` — the caller's identifier never leaves the request; the response contains no pseudonym either. |
  | `profileSection` | no | One of the five ratified sections (`PERSONAL`, `EMPLOYMENT`, `EDUCATION`, `ADDITIONAL`, `PAYROLL`). Omitted = fan out across all five and merge. |
  | `recordIdentity` | no | Passed through to `ResolveEmployeeID`; relevant only for multi-record domains. |

  ## Response body
  | Field | Notes |
  |---|---|
  | `status` | `ok`, `rejected`, or `error`. An empty `history` array with `status: "ok"` is a valid, non-error outcome — "never anchored yet" is not an error. |
  | `history[]` | Newest-first (`Timestamp` string-sorted descending — RFC3339, so lexicographic order is correct). Each entry: `profileSection`, `canonicalizationVersion`, `dataHash`, `hashAlgo`, `ipfsCIDs`, `prevHash`, `recordID`, `timestamp`, `updatedBy`, `version`. |
  | `detail` | Present on `rejected`/`error`. |

  By design, no entry ever carries the section's underlying value or its
  salt — only the anchored digest and its provenance metadata. This is the
  zero-PII-on-chain guarantee surfaced back to the caller, not just an
  on-ledger property.

  ## Status → HTTP code
  | status | HTTP |
  |---|---|
  | `ok` | 200 |
  | `rejected` | 400 |
  | `error` | 500 |
}
